  "query_preview": "..Mid-level ridging continues to build across the Great \nBasin with the departure of the system that brought notable rainfall \nto the forecast area. As such, height rises continue to occur across \nmuch of the western U.S. ushering in a brief period of quiet weather \nthrough Wednesday morning. Cold northwest mid-level flow will \ncontinue pumping cold air into the forecast area, keeping overnight \ntemperatures tonight roughly 5F colder than normal with daytime \nhighs tomorrow running 5-10F below normal. \n\nOn Wednesday, a compact weak shortwave trough will traverse the \nGreat Basin bringing another round of light rain to lower elevation \nlocations and light snowfall to higher terrain locations across \nnorthern and central UT. Given the moisture starved nature of this \nsystem and how quickly it will traverse east with time, no \nsignificant rainfall/snowfall is expected with this system. However, \nit will draw down some colder air keeping temperatures roughly 5-10F \ncooler than normal through Thursday morning. \n\nFollowing this system, strong ridging looks to build in across much \nof the western U.S. keeping conditions moderated through early next \nweek. With persistent height rises throughout the period, a warming \ntrend appears likely starting Saturday, with temperatures warming to \npotentially as high as 5-10F above average across much of northern \nand central UT.",
